Critical Risks and Unnoticed Six Major Gaps:  Why do metro and railway safety inspections miss critical risks? 
Inspections can miss critical risks when they capture a single moment, use checklists that no longer match the work, separate related reports or close actions without checking effectiveness. AI-supported EHS management can help teams review patterns and prioritise follow-up, but it still depends on reliable field evidence and competent human decisions.

A critical risk, in this article, means an exposure with the potential for severe harm that requires effective controls and timely attention. It is not simply whichever item receives the highest automated score.

Six gaps that can leave risk unnoticed
1. The work changes after the inspection
A clear walkway at the start of a shift may become a temporary storage area later. A barrier may be moved for access. Another contractor may enter a work area after the original checks.

A completed inspection records conditions at a particular time. It cannot prove that those conditions remained unchanged. Build checks around meaningful changes in activity as well as the planned inspection schedule.

2. The checklist asks about yesterday’s conditions
Standardisation helps, but a familiar form can become detached from the actual job. A station undergoing refurbishment needs questions that reflect temporary routes and changing work boundaries. A depot maintenance area needs attention to the work and equipment present that day.

Review templates when layouts, activities or controls change. Leave room for inspectors to report a concern that does not fit a predefined question.

3. Small reports remain disconnected
One team records “materials beside access route”. Another reports “restricted passage”. A third raises a near miss in the same area. Read separately, they may appear to be unrelated housekeeping issues.

Read together, they may point to a recurring weakness in how the area is used. Consistent location, activity and hazard information makes that connection easier to investigate.

4. A closed action is mistaken for an effective control
A photograph can show that an obstruction was removed. It does not establish why the obstruction appeared or whether it will return.

Closure should answer two questions: was the agreed action completed, and did it address the problem? For recurring findings, a follow-up during the relevant activity can be more informative than another photograph taken when the area is quiet.

5. Ownership becomes unclear at handover
An inspector identifies an issue. A contractor is expected to resolve it. A supervisor assumes maintenance has accepted it. The next shift sees a note but cannot tell whether temporary controls remain in place.

Each finding needs a responsible owner, a required response, a due time appropriate to the risk and a clear verification route. Shift handovers should make unresolved exposure visible.

6. The dashboard rewards completion more than learning
A high completion rate shows that inspections took place. It does not, by itself, show that serious hazards were controlled.

If teams focus only on closing forms, repeated findings and weak evidence can disappear behind a reassuring percentage. Review what inspections discovered, what changed because of them and what came back.

